const publishedDate = '2026-04-16';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogType="article" canonical="https://sustainabilitydiet.com/field-notes/seed-dispersal" readingBar>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-16">

    <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-3">
      <a href="/field-notes" class="hover:text-ochre-deep">Field Notes</a>
    </p>

    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-xs text-ink/55 mb-6 small-caps tracking-wide">
      <time datetime={publishedDate}>April 2026</time>
      <span>·</span>
      <span>4 min read</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight leading-[1.05] mb-4">
      Seed dispersal and the evolutionary contract
    </h1>
    <p class="font-serif italic text-xl text-ink/70 leading-relaxed mb-10">
      On what fruit is for, and on the mouth it was aimed at.
    </p>

    <hr class="hairline mb-12" />

    <div class="prose-reading max-w-none font-serif text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-6">

      <p class="first-letter:font-display first-letter:text-6xl first-letter:float-left first-letter:leading-[0.9] first-letter:pr-3 first-letter:pt-1">
        When I eat fruit I still swallow the seeds. Their dispersal is unlikely. I half-ass honor my end of the evolutionary contract, nonetheless. And the thought always bugs me that fruit is the only food besides milk that actually wants to be consumed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Almost everything else we eat was trying not to be. A carrot is a storage organ, and being dug up is a failure state. A cow did not want to be slaughtered. A grain of wheat wants to fall on the ground and become another plant — not to be ground into flour. The plant's preference and ours line up only in small, strange cases. Milk. And fruit.
      </p>

      <p>
        Milk is uncomplicated. A mammal produces it for offspring; the contract is with its own species. We are a sideline beneficiary who came in through the back door. Fruit is different. Fruit is an advertisement. The sugar, the color, the smell — all of that is the plant shouting across the canopy: <em>there is a reward in here; carry it somewhere and leave the seed behind when you go</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The contract is simple. You get the pulp. The seed gets a ride to a patch of ground that is not directly beneath the parent tree, where it would be shaded, out-competed, and eventually eaten by something nested at the base. In exchange for the sugar, the plant gets a future in a different place.
      </p>

      <p>
        So when you swallow a seed and then sit down at a desk for the rest of the day, the contract is partially broken. The seed got the ride, but only as far as a porcelain bowl. The plant's descendants do not, in any realistic sense, find a new patch of ground. We honor the part of the contract we can: we make the sugar worthwhile. We keep the fruit industry in business. We keep the plants cultivated. Just — not where they were trying to go.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is one way to read what happened to a particular kind of plant across the Americas about twelve thousand years ago.
      </p>

      <p>
        Go look at an osage orange tree in October, when the fruit is on the ground. It is the size of a softball, bitter, full of a milky latex that no mammal I know of can stomach. A squirrel gnaws through one and abandons the rest. The fruit rots where it fell. The seeds germinate, mostly, right beneath the parent. The tree produces a hundred of these things a year and sends none of them anywhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not a design failure. The contract is unchanged. The tree is still shouting. It is just that the mouth it was aimed at — something the size of a <em>Camelops</em>, with a tough palate, willing to eat a bitter, latex-filled gourd and walk twenty miles before defecating the seeds onto a disturbed river bottom — is gone. The tree is still sending the message. The advertisement is still running. The receiver is just off the air.
      </p>

      <p>
        Kentucky coffeetree is the same. Honey locust is the same. Pawpaw is almost the same. Avocado is a Central American version of the same thing — a fruit so large and single-seeded that it makes no ecological sense unless you assume a gomphothere used to come through.
      </p>

      <p>
        This condition has a name. Daniel Janzen and Paul Martin gave it one in 1982. They called it <em>neotropical anachronism</em>, and their paper is the reason half the rewilding literature exists — because it turned out that once you could see it, you could not unsee it. Half the fruits of the Americas were advertisements for an audience that had left the theater.
      </p>

      <p>
        What bothers me about the half-assed contract is not that I am personally failing it. I am not the target audience. It is that the system still works, just invisibly and badly. Osage orange is not rare. You can drive through Oklahoma and see fencerows of it. But it is not <em>propagating</em>. It is <em>persisting</em>. There is a difference, and the difference is the difference between an ecosystem that functions and one that is on life support without anyone having noticed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The plants kept their end of the contract. They made the fruit. They are still making the fruit. They are sitting down at the table with the sugar and the color and the patient advertisement, and waiting.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not an essay about guilt. I am not the mouth they were aimed at. I cannot be. I can't walk twenty miles after dinner and cannot metabolize bitter latex and would rather not try. The question is whether we can restore the mouth. Not with a resurrection — the original species are not coming back — but with a <a href="/near-time/proxies" class="text-ochre-deep hover:text-ink">proxy</a> close enough to keep the message in circulation. A camel, for example. A camel walking a fence line in Texas, eating the osage orange fruit that the October wind has knocked to the ground, and carrying the seeds into the bottomland.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is not a thought experiment. That is <a href="/one-hundred-camels/the-experiment" class="text-ochre-deep hover:text-ink">a proposal with a budget</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the meantime, I still eat fruit. And I still swallow the seeds. And I think about the contract, and what it would mean to honor it properly, which is to put the fruit into a body that knows what to do with it and set that body down on a piece of ground where the seed has somewhere to go.
      </p>

    </div>

